# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.gitignore` ignores `.env` and `.env.*`, and un-ignores this
# file alone — so the template is tracked and every filled-in copy is not.
#
# Never fill this file in. It is committed. A value written here is published on the next push, and
# a published key is not undone by deleting the file afterwards — rotation is the only remedy.
# `.gitignore:17-20` records that this has already happened once in this repository.

# --- The Supabase project ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both values are on the project's API settings page. Both are PUBLIC BY DESIGN: Vite inlines every
# `VITE_`-prefixed variable into the browser bundle, so anyone who loads the app can read them.
# That is the intended design and not a leak — the URL identifies the project, the anon key
# identifies the project rather than the caller, and what a caller may do is decided entirely by the
# row-level security policies (ADR-005, `.ai/standards/rbac-and-security.md` § Secrets).
#
# The consequence worth reading twice: a policy is the only thing standing between this key and the
# data. There is nothing behind it.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --- The datastore seam --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `mock` | `supabase` — `src/lib/data/index.ts:523`. Leave it UNSET for normal work: the resolver is
#
#     usesMock = VITE_DATA_SEAM === "mock" || !VITE_SUPABASE_URL
#
# so an unset seam with a URL present gives Supabase, and an unset seam with no URL falls back to the
# in-memory mock. `App.tsx` renders `seam-banner` with the answer, deliberately — a build that
# silently fell back to a fake datastore would accept writes into memory and look entirely normal.
#
# Set it to `mock` only to run the app against fixtures with a real project configured.

# VITE_DATA_SEAM=mock

# --- What must NEVER appear in this file, in `.env.local`, or in any `VITE_`-prefixed name ---------
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. It bypasses row-level security entirely, and under ADR-005 that is not
# one control among several — it is the whole authorization model in a single string. It has no use
# in this repository: there is no server, and the browser must never hold it. Migrations and the seed
# are applied by a human through the Supabase SQL editor or the CLI, which carry their own
# credentials (ADR-024).

# --- Not read from here --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The end-to-end suite pins its own seam and does not inherit this file: `playwright.config.ts:49-51`
# sets `VITE_DATA_SEAM=mock` and `VITE_SUPABASE_URL=""` in `webServer.env`, which Playwright merges
# over `process.env` and Vite applies after the parsed `.env`. That pinning is BUG-001, and it is
# what stops `pnpm e2e` on a configured machine from driving the live project with your credentials.
